  "analysis": "SMCI's technical and market structure picture is among the most damaged in the AI infrastructure cohort. The June 10, 2026 session is the defining event: a ~33% single-day collapse on 191M shares — followed immediately by 251M shares on June 11 still failing to recover meaningfully — is not a flush-and-reversal pattern. It is distribution. The two-day combined volume of 442M shares represents approximately 75-80% of estimated float changing hands without price recovery, which is the textbook signature of forced liquidation or a fundamental shock that eliminates the marginal buyer's thesis. [Source: FMP /historical-price-full]\n\nThe unresolved catalyst question (c1712f9a) remains critical. Without knowing whether this was a DOJ/SEC development, a major customer defection, a secondary offering, or an earnings pre-announcement, it is impossible to calibrate whether the sell-off was technically overdone or fundamentally justified. The prior accounting restatement history makes regulatory action the highest-probability explanation for this type of velocity event, but this cannot be confirmed from available data.\n\nFrom a moving average perspective, the damage is complete. SMCI now trades below all four tracked SMAs with the SMA9 ($36.18) and SMA20 ($38.29) having crossed decisively above price — both will serve as resistance in any technical recovery attempt. The SMA50 ($32.57) and SMA200 ($35.67) form a resistance cluster between $32.57 and $35.67 that would need to be cleared before technical buyers return in size. The pre-crash rally from $27.74 (late April) to $50.17 (June 2) — a 81% move in roughly five weeks — now reads as a classic short-squeeze or momentum rally that fully reversed, leaving no meaningful support structure between current price and the mid-$20s. [Source: FMP /historical-price-full]\n\nInstitutional positioning adds further concern. The two anonymous new large holders (CIK 0002100119 and 0002100121) with combined ~62M new shares in